jimmy ly was at last year's tiananmen  square rally in hong kong for just 15  minutes and did not address the crowd  the judge said prosecutors had been able  to prove he had incited others to attend  the vigil lai used to own apple daily a  vocal pro-democracy paper  the tabloid was shut down after police  raided its officers and arrested staff  under a national security law  some journalists in hong kong say they  are not surprised by wednesday's verdict  this has been the trend over the last  year the judge amanda woodcock ruled  that jimmy ly didn't have to say  anything uh to even incite her there's a  very fact that he turned up just outside  the park was quote a deliberate act to  rally support for and publicly spotlight  the unauthorized assembly  the judge also found barrister chao hong  tung and former reporter gwyneth ho  guilty of either taking part or inciting  others to join the event  all three pleaded not guilty  16 other activists are already serving  jail sentences for also taking part in  the commemoration  hong kong has traditionally held large  events on the anniversary of the teleman  crackdown in beijing  for the first time in 30 years police in  hong kong banned last year's rally  citing coronavirus restrictions  commemorations were also banned this  year  this case itself is part i would say  perhaps a wider crackdown on the memory  of the 1989 massacre textbooks have been  censored museum exhibits confiscated  the vigils organizers have been put  behind bars and as you can see this  commemoration  has been banned  last year's ban was imposed just as  china passed a sweeping national  security law in hong kong  activists say the latest convictions are  another example of hong kong's leaders  bowing to pressure from beijing to  censure pro-democracy expression  florence louis al jazeera
 

